1
00:00:04,091 --> 00:00:05,353
Body's inside.

2
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:07,050
Victim's name is
Gabriel Meadows.

3
00:00:07,137 --> 00:00:09,052
Somebody hit him in the back
of the head with a blunt object.

4
00:00:09,139 --> 00:00:10,488
Cleaning crew found
him around 1:00 A.M.

5
00:00:10,575 --> 00:00:12,055
Any witnesses?

6
00:00:12,142 --> 00:00:14,231
Local boys talked to a
co-worker, Dave Bisping

7
00:00:14,318 --> 00:00:15,928
who saw him here at
10:00 last night.

8
00:00:16,016 --> 00:00:17,756
- Rigsby's meeting him at CBI.
- What about the murder weapon?

9
00:00:17,843 --> 00:00:19,106
Nothing inside.

10
00:00:19,193 --> 00:00:20,498
Maybe the killer
dropped it nearby.

11
00:00:20,585 --> 00:00:22,805
Get a group of people together.
Search the area.

12
00:00:22,892 --> 00:00:24,807
Trash duty.

13
00:00:24,894 --> 00:00:27,462
- Is there a problem?
- No. Nothing.

14
00:00:27,549 --> 00:00:30,030
Alright, when Cho
shows up, grab him.

15
00:00:30,117 --> 00:00:32,902
- Spread the pain around.
- Alright.

16
00:00:32,989 --> 00:00:35,426
[instrumental music]

17
00:00:36,819 --> 00:00:38,908
Morning, Lisbon.

18
00:00:38,995 --> 00:00:40,040
Is it morning yet?

19
00:00:40,127 --> 00:00:41,650
Not quite.

20
00:00:41,737 --> 00:00:43,130
Any prints?

21
00:00:43,217 --> 00:00:45,480
None. But it looks
like someone wiped up.

22
00:00:45,567 --> 00:00:47,308
Some of the blood is smears,
not spatter.

23
00:00:47,395 --> 00:00:48,439
Was anything missing?

24
00:00:48,526 --> 00:00:50,093
Looks like his
laptop was taken

25
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:51,660
but whoever took it
was in a hurry.

26
00:00:51,747 --> 00:00:55,446
They left his cell
phone and wallet.

27
00:00:55,533 --> 00:00:56,969
Interesting, yes?

28
00:00:57,057 --> 00:00:58,667
Hmm, because the murder
and the theft

29
00:00:58,754 --> 00:01:00,103
seem hurried, impulsive.

30
00:01:00,190 --> 00:01:02,410
But the cleanup,
methodical and careful.

31
00:01:02,497 --> 00:01:05,239
Almost as if it was carried out
by two different people.

32
00:01:05,326 --> 00:01:09,156
You think somebody else
cleaned up after he was killed?

33
00:01:09,243 --> 00:01:11,027
Why would somebody do that?

34
00:01:11,114 --> 00:01:12,768
Excellent question.

35
00:01:12,855 --> 00:01:14,639
"Anti-Cult activistnetwork."

36
00:01:14,726 --> 00:01:16,989
I've heard of them,
they expose fake religions

37
00:01:17,077 --> 00:01:18,817
rescue cult members,
stuff like that?

38
00:01:18,904 --> 00:01:22,256
Meadows specialized
in one in particular.

39
00:01:22,343 --> 00:01:24,040
"Visualize."
Wait, that's the cult

40
00:01:24,127 --> 00:01:25,172
that Bret Stiles runs.

41
00:01:25,259 --> 00:01:27,130
- You got it.
- Great. Great.

42
00:01:27,217 --> 00:01:28,479
We're dealing with those wackos
again.

43
00:01:28,566 --> 00:01:30,090
"Wackos?"

44
00:01:30,177 --> 00:01:31,743
That's a little
uncharitable, Lisbon.

45
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:33,180
Uncharitable?
What do you mean?

46
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:34,355
Well, I always knew
that you were

47
00:01:34,442 --> 00:01:36,270
closed-minded
about Visualize

48
00:01:36,357 --> 00:01:37,619
but I never thought
you were actually bigoted.

49
00:01:37,706 --> 00:01:38,707
I'm not bigoted.

50
00:01:38,794 --> 00:01:40,448
Of course you
would think that.

51
00:01:40,535 --> 00:01:42,276
Because your
preconceptions blind you.

52
00:01:42,363 --> 00:01:43,625
'Use your eyes.'

53
00:01:43,712 --> 00:01:45,975
Your eyes are
the gateway to truth.

54
00:01:46,062 --> 00:01:47,846
I actually have no idea
what you're talking about.

55
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:50,588
I know you don't,
but he does. Hi.

56
00:01:50,675 --> 00:01:52,068
Excuse me?

57
00:01:52,155 --> 00:01:53,983
You're a member
of Visualize.

58
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:56,028
- No, I'm not.
- Yes, you are.

59
00:01:56,116 --> 00:01:57,639
"Your eyes are
the gateway to truth.."

60
00:01:57,726 --> 00:01:58,901
Visualize dogma.

61
00:01:58,988 --> 00:02:00,468
You're the only one
that recognized it.

62
00:02:00,555 --> 00:02:02,992
Look, I don't know
what you're after.

63
00:02:03,079 --> 00:02:05,821
- I'm just trying to do my job.
- Well, let me explain.

64
00:02:05,908 --> 00:02:08,128
A critic of Visualize
was killed here this evening

65
00:02:08,215 --> 00:02:09,868
and a member shows
up at the scene.

66
00:02:09,955 --> 00:02:11,522
That can't be an accident.

67
00:02:11,609 --> 00:02:13,481
You're the one that
cleared all this up.

68
00:02:13,568 --> 00:02:16,571
- Well, that's crazy.
- I don't think it's crazy.

69
00:02:16,658 --> 00:02:19,008
- What's your name?
- Downs.

70
00:02:19,095 --> 00:02:21,489
Were you involved in this,
Officer Downs?

71
00:02:21,576 --> 00:02:22,968
No. No.

72
00:02:23,055 --> 00:02:24,796
Liar, liar,
pants on fire.

73
00:02:24,883 --> 00:02:28,409
You're coming with us.
We're gonna search your car.

74
00:02:28,496 --> 00:02:32,195
- Stop him! Somebody stop him!
- Whoa! Whoa! Whoa!

75
00:02:32,282 --> 00:02:33,805
Let me go!

76
00:02:33,892 --> 00:02:36,547
- This is religious persecution!
- Let's go.

77
00:02:39,202 --> 00:02:41,291
Pre-member bigot!

78
00:02:41,378 --> 00:02:44,294
Sticks and stones
may break my bones

79
00:02:44,381 --> 00:02:47,341
but names will never hurt me.

80
00:02:47,428 --> 00:02:50,257
[theme music]

81
00:03:04,619 --> 00:03:07,361
We're testing your
uniform for blood

82
00:03:07,448 --> 00:03:09,798
and we found this in the trunk
of your squad car.

83
00:03:14,716 --> 00:03:16,761
Your watch commander
vouched for your whereabouts

84
00:03:16,848 --> 00:03:18,328
at the time of the murder.

85
00:03:18,415 --> 00:03:19,634
We know you didn't
kill Meadows

86
00:03:19,721 --> 00:03:21,201
but you did clean
up the crime scene

87
00:03:21,288 --> 00:03:24,856
making it very difficult
for us to find the killer.

88
00:03:24,943 --> 00:03:26,206
Why would you do that?

89
00:03:31,559 --> 00:03:32,560
Hello?

90
00:03:42,657 --> 00:03:45,703
So, Mr. Bisping, you set up
A-CAN with Meadows, right?

91
00:03:45,790 --> 00:03:47,966
How'd he get into
the anti-cult business?

92
00:03:48,053 --> 00:03:50,360
Uh, Gabe was born
into Visualize.

93
00:03:50,447 --> 00:03:52,841
When his parents
died five years ago

94
00:03:52,928 --> 00:03:55,191
he sort of woke up.
Um..

95
00:03:56,584 --> 00:03:59,326
His younger sister
is still in Visualize.

96
00:03:59,413 --> 00:04:01,502
Everything he did
was about getting her out.

97
00:04:03,068 --> 00:04:04,896
All it did
was get him killed.

98
00:04:04,983 --> 00:04:06,158
Visualize has
a lot of enemies.

99
00:04:06,246 --> 00:04:07,725
Why would they target Gabe?

100
00:04:07,812 --> 00:04:10,075
To shut him up.

101
00:04:10,162 --> 00:04:12,469
Gabe was
a fierce researcher.

102
00:04:12,556 --> 00:04:14,036
He broke a lot of
embarrassing stories

103
00:04:14,123 --> 00:04:17,518
about Visualize
for our web site.

104
00:04:17,605 --> 00:04:18,954
He was hitting
the inner circle hard.

105
00:04:19,041 --> 00:04:20,172
The inner circle?

106
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,958
They help Stiles
run Visualize.

107
00:04:23,045 --> 00:04:25,830
It-it's sort of his
College of Cardinals.

108
00:04:25,917 --> 00:04:27,615
What was Gabe working
on before he died?

109
00:04:27,702 --> 00:04:29,791
Murder.

110
00:04:29,878 --> 00:04:33,751
From the early days
of Visualize back in the '70s.

111
00:04:33,838 --> 00:04:36,014
Gabe thought Stiles
killed a rival of his

112
00:04:36,101 --> 00:04:37,277
to consolidate his power.

113
00:04:37,364 --> 00:04:38,582
He dig up any evidence?

114
00:04:38,669 --> 00:04:41,281
He told me last
week he was close.

115
00:04:41,368 --> 00:04:43,500
Now he's dead.

116
00:04:43,587 --> 00:04:44,588
What do you think?

117
00:04:45,937 --> 00:04:47,330
[telephone ringing]

118
00:04:47,417 --> 00:04:49,201
How's the spy?

119
00:04:49,289 --> 00:04:50,986
He hasn't moved since
we brought him in.

120
00:04:51,073 --> 00:04:53,162
- It's creepy.
- Well, it's not surprising.

121
00:04:53,249 --> 00:04:55,817
He's a member of
a brainwashing cult.

122
00:04:55,904 --> 00:04:57,384
The thought of a clean mind

123
00:04:57,471 --> 00:04:59,255
is terribly
frightening to you.

124
00:04:59,342 --> 00:05:00,517
Isn't it, Patrick?

125
00:05:00,604 --> 00:05:02,780
Bret Stiles.

126
00:05:02,867 --> 00:05:05,305
The brainwasher-in-chief.

127
00:05:05,392 --> 00:05:08,482
Ah, Agent Lisbon,
it's been far too long.

128
00:05:08,569 --> 00:05:11,093
Why are you here,
Mr. Stiles?

129
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:14,705
I'm the spiritual advisor
of a man that you've imprisoned

130
00:05:14,792 --> 00:05:16,881
and I was told
that Patrick here

131
00:05:16,968 --> 00:05:18,361
tricked him into revealing

132
00:05:18,448 --> 00:05:19,971
that he was a member
of my church

133
00:05:20,058 --> 00:05:22,322
which, of course,
is not a crime.

134
00:05:22,409 --> 00:05:24,715
He tampered with evidence.
That is a crime.

135
00:05:24,802 --> 00:05:27,283
Well, if you let me
talk to him, Agent Lisbon

136
00:05:27,370 --> 00:05:28,676
I'm sure we can clear
all this up.

137
00:05:28,763 --> 00:05:31,766
Uh, what's going on, Bret?

138
00:05:31,853 --> 00:05:33,855
- Hmm?
- They're not Visualize members.

139
00:05:33,942 --> 00:05:35,378
They're professional muscle.

140
00:05:35,465 --> 00:05:36,901
You don't trust your own
people to protect you.

141
00:05:36,988 --> 00:05:38,468
What are you scared of?

142
00:05:38,555 --> 00:05:39,991
I wouldn't want you
to mistake me

143
00:05:40,078 --> 00:05:41,819
for Red John
and shoot me.

144
00:05:43,691 --> 00:05:46,607
[intense music]

145
00:05:46,694 --> 00:05:48,565
Look at me.

146
00:05:50,567 --> 00:05:52,308
Tell them everything.

147
00:05:55,703 --> 00:05:58,923
I heard a report of
the body over my radio.

148
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:02,449
Got to the office before
the first responders.

149
00:06:02,536 --> 00:06:04,233
Wiped the surfaces
for fingerprints

150
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:05,539
wiped up some blood, too.

151
00:06:05,626 --> 00:06:08,498
- Why?
- Meadows was an ex-member.

152
00:06:08,585 --> 00:06:10,718
A non-person.

153
00:06:10,805 --> 00:06:15,026
Whoever killed him was
doing Visualize a service.

154
00:06:15,113 --> 00:06:17,333
You were duty-bound to
help clean up the evidence

155
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:18,639
weren't you?

156
00:06:21,293 --> 00:06:25,123
No. I did it
of my own initiative.

157
00:06:25,210 --> 00:06:28,257
You know, as a rule,
we hope to welcome back

158
00:06:28,344 --> 00:06:29,998
'ex-members into the fold.'

159
00:06:30,085 --> 00:06:32,740
We certainly don't want
to aid their assassins.

160
00:06:32,827 --> 00:06:35,873
What about the missing laptop?
Did you take that?

161
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,484
No.

162
00:06:37,571 --> 00:06:40,574
The computer was already
gone when I got there.

163
00:06:40,661 --> 00:06:43,228
Hmm.
That's the truth.

164
00:06:43,315 --> 00:06:45,883
[sighs]
Alright.
Take him to booking

165
00:06:49,713 --> 00:06:52,760
Well, I've done
my good deed for the day.

166
00:06:52,847 --> 00:06:54,631
If there's nothing else,
I'll be getting along.

167
00:06:54,718 --> 00:06:56,241
We just have
a few more questions.

168
00:06:56,328 --> 00:06:58,200
Well, uh, could we
do it on the walk?

169
00:06:58,287 --> 00:07:00,811
Uh, I've gotta get back
to running my religion.

170
00:07:00,898 --> 00:07:03,074
Yes, your flock
won't fleece itself.

171
00:07:03,161 --> 00:07:07,122
Mr. Stiles, where were you last
night between 10:00 and 1:00?

172
00:07:07,209 --> 00:07:09,646
What, uh, during the murder?
Let me see.

173
00:07:09,733 --> 00:07:11,474
I was at Casper's Bistro.

174
00:07:11,561 --> 00:07:14,042
Had the roasted turbot.
It was a little dry.

175
00:07:14,129 --> 00:07:16,871
We'll check, I'm also gonna
have Agent Rigsby get in contact

176
00:07:16,958 --> 00:07:18,307
with your people to see what
your schedule was yesterday.

177
00:07:18,394 --> 00:07:20,091
Of course.

178
00:07:20,178 --> 00:07:23,530
Uh, I have one last question.

179
00:07:23,617 --> 00:07:26,184
You did this
for a lowly drone.

180
00:07:26,271 --> 00:07:27,795
Why is that?

181
00:07:27,882 --> 00:07:31,015
Every man in Visualize
is like a son to me.

182
00:07:32,408 --> 00:07:34,323
Every woman
is my daughter.

183
00:07:34,410 --> 00:07:36,978
Ooh. How are those
demons of yours

184
00:07:37,065 --> 00:07:38,980
coming on, Patrick?

185
00:07:39,067 --> 00:07:40,938
They're still chasing you,
are they?

186
00:07:42,897 --> 00:07:44,159
I'm chasing them.

187
00:07:45,769 --> 00:07:46,944
Are you?

188
00:07:49,207 --> 00:07:52,254
Seriously, I haven't had
hot water in three days.

189
00:07:52,341 --> 00:07:56,911
My landlord's being a total
jerk and won't fix it.

190
00:07:56,998 --> 00:07:58,782
You should buy.

191
00:07:58,869 --> 00:08:00,784
So I can deal with a bank
instead of a landlord?

192
00:08:00,871 --> 00:08:02,177
Big step up.

193
00:08:02,264 --> 00:08:04,353
Excuse me, agents?
We found something.

194
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:05,702
You should take a look.

195
00:08:08,966 --> 00:08:10,664
They were stuffed
inside this old TV.

196
00:08:10,751 --> 00:08:13,362
We almost missed 'em.

197
00:08:13,449 --> 00:08:14,668
Ooh, a lot of blood here.

198
00:08:17,497 --> 00:08:19,368
That suit
looks handmade.

199
00:08:19,455 --> 00:08:21,196
If the tailor's local,
maybe we can ID the owner.

200
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:23,807
"Gerard's on Sutter."

201
00:08:23,894 --> 00:08:25,940
- That's in San Fran, isn't it?
- Yeah.

202
00:08:28,725 --> 00:08:31,772
What are you reading?
"Visualize Self."

203
00:08:31,859 --> 00:08:32,903
You thinking about converting?

204
00:08:32,990 --> 00:08:34,775
Yeah, just brushing up.

205
00:08:34,862 --> 00:08:37,125
Well, Stiles might have to
write his next book from jail.

206
00:08:37,212 --> 00:08:38,561
His alibi didn't hold up.

207
00:08:38,648 --> 00:08:40,084
He left the restaurant
at 10:30.

208
00:08:40,171 --> 00:08:41,999
- Well, that's not like him.
- To lie?

209
00:08:42,086 --> 00:08:45,568
- No, to do it so poorly.
- Van Pelt and Cho called in.

210
00:08:45,655 --> 00:08:48,832
Tailor identified the clothes.
They belong to Bret Stiles.

211
00:08:48,919 --> 00:08:52,096
Alright, we're dealing with the
head of a multi-national cult

212
00:08:52,183 --> 00:08:53,402
a 900-pound gorilla.

213
00:08:53,489 --> 00:08:56,710
We need to proceed
carefully and slowly.

214
00:08:56,797 --> 00:08:58,189
Put together a timeline
of the day of the murder.

215
00:08:58,276 --> 00:08:59,756
I wanna know where
he was every minute.

216
00:08:59,843 --> 00:09:02,542
- You got it.
- We should pay Bret a visit.

217
00:09:02,629 --> 00:09:04,239
Not until we have
an airtight case.

218
00:09:04,326 --> 00:09:06,371
Where's the fun in that?

219
00:09:06,458 --> 00:09:09,157
Let's get a stick and go
and poke this 900-pound gorilla.

220
00:09:09,244 --> 00:09:11,638
See what happens.

221
00:09:11,725 --> 00:09:13,901
[instrumental music]

222
00:09:19,254 --> 00:09:21,343
Mr. Stiles.

223
00:09:21,430 --> 00:09:23,171
Oh, Patrick. Agent Lisbon.

224
00:09:23,258 --> 00:09:24,868
How nice of you
to join us.

225
00:09:24,955 --> 00:09:27,567
This is Cal Leben
and Jason Cooper.

226
00:09:27,654 --> 00:09:30,004
Two of my most
loyal lieutenants.

227
00:09:30,091 --> 00:09:33,964
Your alibi for the Meadows
killing didn't hold up.

228
00:09:34,051 --> 00:09:35,487
You left that restaurant
hours earlier

229
00:09:35,575 --> 00:09:37,446
than you told me you did.

230
00:09:37,533 --> 00:09:40,623
Really? I must have been
mistaken. I'm sorry.

231
00:09:40,710 --> 00:09:43,408
And, uh, we found bloody
clothes in a dumpster

232
00:09:43,495 --> 00:09:44,758
'near the crime scene.'

233
00:09:44,845 --> 00:09:46,803
Bloody clothes
that belong to you.

234
00:09:46,890 --> 00:09:49,806
Listen, I give clothes
away all the time

235
00:09:49,893 --> 00:09:51,329
to Visualize charities.

236
00:09:51,416 --> 00:09:52,940
Yeah, I'm sure you do.

237
00:09:53,027 --> 00:09:54,681
[phone ringing]

238
00:09:54,768 --> 00:09:56,030
Would you excuse me?

239
00:09:57,161 --> 00:09:58,510
Rigsby, what's up?

240
00:10:01,470 --> 00:10:02,645
What's going on, Bret?

241
00:10:03,690 --> 00:10:05,605
Beg your pardon?

242
00:10:05,692 --> 00:10:07,607
You know this doesn't look good,
yet you're not asking

243
00:10:07,694 --> 00:10:08,999
the questions that you should.

244
00:10:10,392 --> 00:10:11,654
Huh?

245
00:10:11,741 --> 00:10:13,787
Why is it that
you're not worried?

246
00:10:13,874 --> 00:10:16,920
What does Bret Stiles
have to be worried about?

247
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:20,620
Well, he's about to get
arrested for murder.

248
00:10:22,970 --> 00:10:24,754
- Bret, what's he talking about?
- Let it go.

249
00:10:27,017 --> 00:10:29,237
- Do you recognize this?
- Well. Let me see. Ooh.

250
00:10:29,324 --> 00:10:32,283
Well, I suppose it could
be me giving a speech.

251
00:10:32,370 --> 00:10:34,198
Aha.
I give lots of speeches.

252
00:10:34,285 --> 00:10:36,723
It was taken yesterday,
and you're wearing

253
00:10:36,810 --> 00:10:39,029
the shirt and tie
we found covered in blood.

254
00:10:42,511 --> 00:10:43,860
Bret Stiles,
you're under arrest

255
00:10:43,947 --> 00:10:45,993
for the murder of
Gabriel Meadows.

256
00:10:46,080 --> 00:10:47,734
You have to come with us.
Let's go.

257
00:10:55,263 --> 00:10:56,699
I said let's go.

258
00:11:00,921 --> 00:11:02,879
[chairs creaking]

259
00:11:07,884 --> 00:11:10,757
[intense music]

260
00:11:27,817 --> 00:11:30,690
[music continues]

261
00:11:31,821 --> 00:11:33,605
Uh..

262
00:11:33,693 --> 00:11:37,044
Everyone, no cause for alarm.

263
00:11:38,175 --> 00:11:40,482
Be at peace.

264
00:11:42,266 --> 00:11:44,312
- Thank you.
- Yes, sir.

265
00:11:45,269 --> 00:11:48,055
[music continues]

266
00:11:59,501 --> 00:12:02,504
Yes.
That appears to be mine.

267
00:12:05,812 --> 00:12:06,943
Where did the blood
come from?

268
00:12:08,031 --> 00:12:09,729
I don't know.

269
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:15,082
Meadows was writing an
article on Timothy Farragut.

270
00:12:15,169 --> 00:12:17,040
He thought you'd
murdered him.

271
00:12:17,127 --> 00:12:19,086
Poor old Tim.

272
00:12:19,173 --> 00:12:21,958
He died, oh,
30 years ago now.

273
00:12:22,045 --> 00:12:23,786
Car crash on PCH.

274
00:12:23,873 --> 00:12:25,962
And what if Meadows
found evidence

275
00:12:26,049 --> 00:12:28,269
that it wasn't an accident?

276
00:12:28,356 --> 00:12:30,575
That would give you motive
for murder, wouldn't it?

277
00:12:30,662 --> 00:12:33,491
No.
Because there's nothing to find.

278
00:12:33,578 --> 00:12:35,450
Look, I'm not responsible
for Tim's death

279
00:12:35,537 --> 00:12:37,669
or the demise
of Mr. Meadows.

280
00:12:37,757 --> 00:12:39,759
Then why does the
evidence say you are?

281
00:12:41,456 --> 00:12:43,284
Well, maybe
I'm being set up.

282
00:12:43,371 --> 00:12:45,939
And why would
somebody do that?

283
00:12:46,026 --> 00:12:48,245
Tell me, Agent Lisbon.

284
00:12:48,332 --> 00:12:50,900
Do you enjoy watching
Patrick Jane

285
00:12:50,987 --> 00:12:53,381
take over your team
and your life?

286
00:12:53,468 --> 00:12:55,252
We close cases.

287
00:12:55,339 --> 00:12:57,385
That's all that
matters to me.

288
00:12:57,472 --> 00:12:59,691
Oh, I see.

289
00:12:59,779 --> 00:13:03,565
So you're telling me that you
love justice more than yourself?

290
00:13:03,652 --> 00:13:05,785
Come on.
Justice, what-what is that?

291
00:13:05,872 --> 00:13:07,482
It's just an idea.

292
00:13:07,569 --> 00:13:11,791
You are flesh and blood.

293
00:13:11,878 --> 00:13:14,968
Why sacrifice the real
for the imaginary?

294
00:13:15,055 --> 00:13:17,666
I'm not looking
for a guru.

295
00:13:17,753 --> 00:13:21,104
- I'm looking for a murderer.
- Hmm.

296
00:13:21,191 --> 00:13:23,106
Well..

297
00:13:23,193 --> 00:13:24,586
...I didn't kill anybody.

298
00:13:24,673 --> 00:13:28,155
And if you keep
insisting that I did then..

299
00:13:28,242 --> 00:13:31,506
...gonna have to lawyer up.

300
00:13:31,593 --> 00:13:33,421
[telephone ringing]

301
00:13:33,508 --> 00:13:34,901
He's lawyered up.

302
00:13:34,988 --> 00:13:36,380
It doesn't give us
much time to nail him.

303
00:13:36,467 --> 00:13:38,295
Where are we at with
the bloody clothes?

304
00:13:38,382 --> 00:13:40,689
Lab confirmed the blood type is
A-Negative, same as the victim

305
00:13:40,776 --> 00:13:42,822
but they won't have the DNA
results for three or four days.

306
00:13:42,909 --> 00:13:44,954
We don't have that much time.
We need something else.

307
00:13:45,041 --> 00:13:47,304
We're still missing the murder
weapon and the victim's laptop.

308
00:13:47,391 --> 00:13:48,871
We're gonna need to search
his house and office.

309
00:13:48,958 --> 00:13:50,264
- I'll get a warrant.
- Good.

310
00:13:50,351 --> 00:13:51,918
Where are we at with
the Farragut motive?

311
00:13:52,005 --> 00:13:53,180
Well, apparently,
his notes were on the laptop

312
00:13:53,267 --> 00:13:54,529
so that's no good.

313
00:13:54,616 --> 00:13:55,747
I have a file of his stories.

314
00:13:55,835 --> 00:13:57,184
Meadows definitely
had a solid contact

315
00:13:57,271 --> 00:13:59,055
feeding him inside
information about Visualize.

316
00:13:59,142 --> 00:14:00,796
Any idea on how
to find the source?

317
00:14:00,883 --> 00:14:02,754
Yeah, there's an old hippie
named Randall Parker.

318
00:14:02,842 --> 00:14:03,799
He's an ex-Visualize member.

319
00:14:03,886 --> 00:14:05,322
Meadows talked to him last week.

320
00:14:05,409 --> 00:14:07,672
Start with him.
What about Meadows' sister?

321
00:14:07,759 --> 00:14:09,761
She's at Clear View ,
the Visualize College.

322
00:14:09,849 --> 00:14:11,546
Visualize has a college?

323
00:14:11,633 --> 00:14:13,026
Apparently, you major
in yourself.

324
00:14:16,725 --> 00:14:18,335
Ah, Patrick.

325
00:14:18,422 --> 00:14:20,511
I wondered when
you'd pop in.

326
00:14:20,598 --> 00:14:22,078
Hmm.

327
00:14:22,165 --> 00:14:24,037
Reading about you.

328
00:14:24,124 --> 00:14:27,083
Gabe Meadows doesn't paint
a very pretty picture.

329
00:14:27,170 --> 00:14:31,044
Oh, surely you're not going
to fall forgutter journalism.

330
00:14:31,131 --> 00:14:33,394
One thing that's consistent
in all these stories

331
00:14:33,481 --> 00:14:36,136
is that you never actually
wield the knife yourself.

332
00:14:36,223 --> 00:14:40,183
You always manipulate someone
else into doing your dirty work.

333
00:14:40,270 --> 00:14:42,577
That's somethingyou'd
know a little bit about.

334
00:14:42,664 --> 00:14:45,710
Getting Red John to kill
the San Joaquin killer

335
00:14:45,797 --> 00:14:46,929
that was very nice.

336
00:14:47,016 --> 00:14:50,193
Almost elegant, I'd say.

337
00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:52,065
Yes, you're coming along.

338
00:14:52,152 --> 00:14:54,241
I'm doing nothing
of this sort.

339
00:14:56,504 --> 00:15:00,377
You're not very happy,
are you, Patrick?

340
00:15:00,464 --> 00:15:04,251
Perhaps you feel a bit like
the sorcerer's apprentice

341
00:15:04,338 --> 00:15:08,777
you've woken up a power
that you can't control.

342
00:15:08,864 --> 00:15:11,345
Tell me, how is
Rosalind Harker

343
00:15:11,432 --> 00:15:13,869
enjoying life under
FBI protection?

344
00:15:13,956 --> 00:15:17,307
Is she glad that you brought
Red John back into her life?

345
00:15:19,788 --> 00:15:22,008
Are you enjoying this?

346
00:15:22,095 --> 00:15:24,880
'Cause I know you didn't
kill Gabe Meadows.

347
00:15:24,967 --> 00:15:26,969
I've been telling
you that all along.

348
00:15:27,056 --> 00:15:30,930
But you're not acting like
a man that's being framed.

349
00:15:31,017 --> 00:15:33,323
Even someone in your position
is gonna reach out for help

350
00:15:33,410 --> 00:15:36,544
when being framed.

351
00:15:36,631 --> 00:15:38,894
Jon Musashi.
I'm Mr. Stiles' attorney.

352
00:15:38,981 --> 00:15:39,982
This interview is over.

353
00:15:43,116 --> 00:15:45,553
Hi, Jon.

354
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:48,469
Are you a member of Visualize?
Yes, I think you are.

355
00:15:48,556 --> 00:15:51,951
Did you know that Mr. Stiles'
last lawyer was a murderer?

356
00:15:52,038 --> 00:15:53,996
Whereas I am not.

357
00:15:54,083 --> 00:15:55,955
And again,
this interview is over.

358
00:15:56,042 --> 00:15:57,826
Excellent.

359
00:15:57,913 --> 00:15:59,959
Well, if you'll excuse me
always a pleasure.

360
00:16:00,046 --> 00:16:01,090
Nice to meet you, Jon

361
00:16:02,048 --> 00:16:04,137
Jon..

362
00:16:04,224 --> 00:16:05,486
Thank you for coming.

363
00:16:05,573 --> 00:16:07,140
- Thank you--
- Mr. Stiles, please.

364
00:16:07,227 --> 00:16:08,184
[Stiles]
'I need to tell
them everything.'

365
00:16:08,271 --> 00:16:10,186
'But I mean, you know me.'

366
00:16:12,667 --> 00:16:15,409
I mean, I'm the leader
of a church, I mean..

367
00:16:19,065 --> 00:16:21,981
[church bell ringing]

368
00:16:27,595 --> 00:16:29,379
Really, I'm okay.

369
00:16:29,466 --> 00:16:31,512
I'm glad to hear it.

370
00:16:31,599 --> 00:16:33,470
No, I mean you can
go back to class.

371
00:16:33,557 --> 00:16:37,039
Oh, my inner visions
don't start for another hour.

372
00:16:37,126 --> 00:16:39,694
Agent Lisbon.
Dean Nora Hill.

373
00:16:39,781 --> 00:16:41,739
Ah, thank you for taking
the time to meet me.

374
00:16:41,826 --> 00:16:43,611
Happy to assist.
That will be all, Rose.

375
00:16:43,698 --> 00:16:45,308
Thank you, Dean Hill.

376
00:16:45,395 --> 00:16:48,268
Jessie Meadows is waiting
for you in Stiles Hall.

377
00:16:48,355 --> 00:16:49,704
It's an impressive campus.

378
00:16:49,791 --> 00:16:51,010
Thank you.

379
00:16:51,097 --> 00:16:55,710
4,642 students
from 27 countries.

380
00:16:55,797 --> 00:16:58,408
Only the best and
the brightest young people.

381
00:16:58,495 --> 00:17:00,497
How are the students
taking the news?

382
00:17:00,584 --> 00:17:01,977
What news?

383
00:17:02,064 --> 00:17:04,153
That one of the students'
brother's been murdered

384
00:17:04,240 --> 00:17:06,764
and your leader's been
arrested for the crime?

385
00:17:06,851 --> 00:17:09,463
They don't know about that.
They don't need to.

386
00:17:09,550 --> 00:17:11,204
But it's all over
the internet.

387
00:17:11,291 --> 00:17:13,815
They don't have
the internet.

388
00:17:13,902 --> 00:17:17,688
The mind suffers from junk
food as much as the body does.

389
00:17:17,775 --> 00:17:18,776
We keep it away.

390
00:17:20,213 --> 00:17:22,519
Does Jessie know
her brother's dead?

391
00:17:22,606 --> 00:17:24,043
Aren't you gonna
tell her?

392
00:17:26,436 --> 00:17:29,700
[Lisbon]
'We'll do whatever we can
to find out who did this.'

393
00:17:29,787 --> 00:17:31,876
I am so sorry
for your loss.

394
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:35,054
Oh, Gabe.

395
00:17:40,885 --> 00:17:42,191
I'm sorry he's dead

396
00:17:42,278 --> 00:17:44,454
but he wasn't a part
of my life anymore.

397
00:17:44,541 --> 00:17:46,239
He's still your brother.

398
00:17:46,326 --> 00:17:48,154
He left me.

399
00:17:48,241 --> 00:17:50,678
Left us.

400
00:17:50,765 --> 00:17:52,071
That's not what
a brother does.

401
00:17:56,292 --> 00:17:58,642
Can you think of anybody
who may have wanted to hurt him?

402
00:18:01,689 --> 00:18:04,300
Gabe published stories
about our community.

403
00:18:04,387 --> 00:18:07,042
Some of the students here,
they didn't like it.

404
00:18:07,129 --> 00:18:09,349
Some of them even thought that
I was feeding him information.

405
00:18:09,436 --> 00:18:12,917
- Were you?
- No, I wouldn't do that.

406
00:18:13,004 --> 00:18:15,616
I know you and your
brother didn't get along.

407
00:18:15,703 --> 00:18:18,184
And he wanted to help you.

408
00:18:18,271 --> 00:18:22,753
I think he wanted that
more than anything.

409
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:26,322
That was the problem.
I didn't need his help.

410
00:18:26,409 --> 00:18:27,584
He couldn't see that.

411
00:18:32,023 --> 00:18:34,025
I'm with my real family here.

412
00:18:38,639 --> 00:18:40,858
[engine revving]

413
00:18:46,821 --> 00:18:48,214
[Rigsby]
'Thanks for seeing me,
Mr. Parker.'

414
00:18:48,301 --> 00:18:50,825
Meadows broke a lot of
stories about Visualize.

415
00:18:50,912 --> 00:18:52,261
Were you his source?

416
00:18:52,348 --> 00:18:55,960
All my stories are
30 years old.

417
00:18:56,047 --> 00:18:59,355
All I know about
are the old days.

418
00:18:59,442 --> 00:19:00,965
Did he ask you about
Timothy Farragut?

419
00:19:01,052 --> 00:19:03,316
- Sure.
- What'd you tell him?

420
00:19:03,403 --> 00:19:04,795
Well, Farragut
was into dispelling

421
00:19:04,882 --> 00:19:08,756
all the bad vibes
in the world.

422
00:19:08,843 --> 00:19:10,975
I liked how that sounded.

423
00:19:11,062 --> 00:19:12,629
A lot of people did.

424
00:19:12,716 --> 00:19:15,589
People who felt guilty
about having too much money.

425
00:19:15,676 --> 00:19:17,199
When did Bret Stiles
come into the picture?

426
00:19:17,286 --> 00:19:18,896
'76 or so.

427
00:19:18,983 --> 00:19:22,073
A year later, Farragut died,
and Stiles wastheman.

428
00:19:22,161 --> 00:19:23,336
I read the police report.

429
00:19:23,423 --> 00:19:24,598
Farragut's death seems like

430
00:19:24,685 --> 00:19:26,077
an open-and-shut
drunk driving fatality.

431
00:19:26,165 --> 00:19:28,993
Well, you notice
who wrote that report.

432
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:31,561
Sheriff Elliott Elswit.

433
00:19:31,648 --> 00:19:34,347
Oh, he liked those
Visualize ladies

434
00:19:34,434 --> 00:19:36,653
and Stiles made sure
he met plenty.

435
00:19:36,740 --> 00:19:39,003
So you're saying that
the sheriff doctored the report?

436
00:19:39,090 --> 00:19:41,180
Well, Farragut didn't drink.

437
00:19:41,267 --> 00:19:44,183
How'd he get so loaded
he couldn't see a tree?

438
00:19:44,270 --> 00:19:45,749
How do I get in touch
with the sheriff?

439
00:19:45,836 --> 00:19:47,925
Elswit's been
dead 20 years.

440
00:19:48,012 --> 00:19:49,536
A suicide, they say.

441
00:19:49,623 --> 00:19:51,538
And you think that Bret Stiles
killed him, too?

442
00:19:51,625 --> 00:19:52,930
You bet I do.

443
00:19:54,845 --> 00:19:56,543
- You think I'm crazy?
- No.

444
00:19:56,630 --> 00:19:58,806
It's just a lot
of conspiracy talk.

445
00:19:58,893 --> 00:20:00,721
Well, just because
you're paranoid

446
00:20:00,808 --> 00:20:02,157
don't mean they're
not after you.

447
00:20:03,202 --> 00:20:04,681
Hey.
Where are you?

448
00:20:04,768 --> 00:20:08,250
- I'm back at the snake pit.
- Visualize? Why?

449
00:20:08,337 --> 00:20:10,383
[Jane]
'Okay, so the Visualize
attorney shows up'

450
00:20:10,470 --> 00:20:13,124
and Stiles starts acting
all weird and scared.

451
00:20:13,212 --> 00:20:14,561
It's just like a little show.

452
00:20:14,648 --> 00:20:17,085
- Hey, could you hold on?
- Yeah.

453
00:20:17,172 --> 00:20:19,783
Uh, would you mind giving
me some privacy, please?

454
00:20:24,397 --> 00:20:25,920
Really? Go.

455
00:20:29,184 --> 00:20:30,881
So Stiles is acting scared.
So?

456
00:20:30,968 --> 00:20:33,144
Well, it's for
the benefit of the lawyer.

457
00:20:33,232 --> 00:20:35,190
Whatever game he's playing,
it isn't aimed at us.

458
00:20:35,277 --> 00:20:37,888
It's aimed at Visualize,
and I'm here to find out why.

459
00:20:37,975 --> 00:20:39,673
- I'll call you when I get out.
- 'Okay.'

460
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:41,152
Bye.

461
00:20:42,153 --> 00:20:45,069
[instrumental music]

462
00:20:45,156 --> 00:20:46,636
Leben.

463
00:20:48,508 --> 00:20:49,683
Mr. Jane.

464
00:20:49,770 --> 00:20:51,467
Hope the whole
"Arrest your master" thing

465
00:20:51,554 --> 00:20:52,816
didn't ruin your lunch.

466
00:20:54,427 --> 00:20:55,515
No hard feelings, right?

467
00:20:55,602 --> 00:20:57,299
Did you come here to gloat?

468
00:20:57,386 --> 00:21:00,737
No, I can gloat from a distance.
I have a message from Stiles.

469
00:21:00,824 --> 00:21:02,913
- Hmm. What is it?
- Sorry.

470
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:04,480
It's for the entire
inner circle.

471
00:21:04,567 --> 00:21:05,786
Ah, you can give it to me.

472
00:21:05,873 --> 00:21:08,005
I'll pass it to
the other members.

473
00:21:08,092 --> 00:21:11,182
Yeah, I-I don't think so.
I think I need the whole gang.

474
00:21:12,836 --> 00:21:14,490
Light the Bret signal.

475
00:21:14,577 --> 00:21:16,971
Gather the pooh-bahs.

476
00:21:17,058 --> 00:21:18,320
Come on.
Let's go.

477
00:21:28,896 --> 00:21:31,202
Ah..

478
00:21:31,290 --> 00:21:35,816
Agent Grace Van Pelt,
I presume.

479
00:21:35,903 --> 00:21:38,775
Mr. Stiles, I'm hoping you can
help me with something.

480
00:21:38,862 --> 00:21:41,300
I'm trying to find
any property

481
00:21:41,387 --> 00:21:43,040
held by you in the
state of California.

482
00:21:43,127 --> 00:21:44,999
A car, a residence?

483
00:21:45,086 --> 00:21:48,916
- Please, Agent Grace Van Pelt.
- Excuse me?

484
00:21:49,003 --> 00:21:50,961
The helpless maiden routine?

485
00:21:52,789 --> 00:21:55,879
Worth a try. Look, I can't
find anything you own.

486
00:21:55,966 --> 00:21:58,317
Well, that's because
there's nothing to find.

487
00:21:58,404 --> 00:22:01,363
I have no house, no car.

488
00:22:01,450 --> 00:22:03,757
certainly not
in myname. Hmm?

489
00:22:03,844 --> 00:22:06,237
I've dedicated my
life to Visualize.

490
00:22:06,325 --> 00:22:08,022
So there's no place
for us to look?

491
00:22:08,109 --> 00:22:10,764
Oh, no. You can look and look.

492
00:22:10,851 --> 00:22:13,027
You will not find.

493
00:22:24,995 --> 00:22:27,215
You're upset.

494
00:22:28,869 --> 00:22:31,480
Something small, domestic?

495
00:22:31,567 --> 00:22:34,004
A household problem?

496
00:22:34,091 --> 00:22:35,658
No.

497
00:22:35,745 --> 00:22:39,183
Hmm? But no, it's not family.

498
00:22:39,270 --> 00:22:44,363
But still, it's...
connected to the home.

499
00:22:44,450 --> 00:22:46,016
Landlord.

500
00:22:48,845 --> 00:22:50,586
Petty dictator, no doubt.

501
00:22:50,673 --> 00:22:52,632
Abuses you because he can.

502
00:22:52,719 --> 00:22:55,896
Or because he's
a jerk. He won't fix..

503
00:23:00,683 --> 00:23:01,945
You're good.

504
00:23:02,032 --> 00:23:03,730
Not as good as Jane,
but good.

505
00:23:03,817 --> 00:23:06,210
Hmm.

506
00:23:06,297 --> 00:23:09,779
The anger that you
feel is a symptom

507
00:23:09,866 --> 00:23:11,694
of a deeper condition

508
00:23:11,781 --> 00:23:14,523
an organic rage, if you will.

509
00:23:14,610 --> 00:23:16,220
'You know..'

510
00:23:18,527 --> 00:23:21,225
I can help you with that.

511
00:23:21,312 --> 00:23:22,575
Not interested.

512
00:23:22,662 --> 00:23:24,751
Hmm?

513
00:23:24,838 --> 00:23:26,666
Close your eyes.

514
00:23:28,102 --> 00:23:30,321
Come on. Close your eyes.

515
00:23:30,409 --> 00:23:35,152
Visualize your anger as
a red cloud inside your body.

516
00:23:35,239 --> 00:23:37,024
Take some deep breaths.

517
00:23:37,111 --> 00:23:38,808
Exhale.

518
00:23:38,895 --> 00:23:41,594
Let the red smoke
come out of your body.

519
00:23:44,597 --> 00:23:45,815
Try it.

520
00:23:45,902 --> 00:23:47,817
Red smoke?

521
00:23:47,904 --> 00:23:49,689
Seriously?

522
00:23:49,776 --> 00:23:51,255
Or not.

523
00:23:51,342 --> 00:23:53,214
Your choice.

524
00:23:54,737 --> 00:23:57,610
[instrumental music]

525
00:24:10,187 --> 00:24:12,799
[door screeching]

526
00:24:23,679 --> 00:24:26,726
Mr. Jane, you have
a message for us from Bret?

527
00:24:26,813 --> 00:24:30,077
Uh, no, not really, no.

528
00:24:30,164 --> 00:24:32,209
Do you mind if I sit here?
Just can't resist.

529
00:24:32,296 --> 00:24:33,602
Looks too comfortable.

530
00:24:35,648 --> 00:24:37,127
Wow.

531
00:24:43,699 --> 00:24:45,440
I don't understand.

532
00:24:45,527 --> 00:24:48,225
We were told you had a message.

533
00:24:48,312 --> 00:24:49,705
No, that was a lie.

534
00:24:49,792 --> 00:24:52,142
I just wanted to get
you all together. Sorry.

535
00:24:54,536 --> 00:24:56,277
Why did you need
to get in here?

536
00:24:56,364 --> 00:24:58,279
Get the lay of the land see
who's running the show

537
00:24:58,366 --> 00:25:00,237
now that Bret's in the clink.

538
00:25:00,324 --> 00:25:02,631
None of us would presume to.

539
00:25:02,718 --> 00:25:04,894
Bret Stiles is
still our leader.

540
00:25:04,981 --> 00:25:06,722
Other members of the inner
circle are flying in.

541
00:25:06,809 --> 00:25:09,638
We hold a vote
reaffirming Bret tomorrow.

542
00:25:12,206 --> 00:25:14,251
Is that what you
came here to find out?

543
00:25:16,166 --> 00:25:19,169
I came here to accuse one
of you of framing Bret Stiles

544
00:25:19,256 --> 00:25:22,042
for the murder
of Gabriel Meadows.

545
00:25:22,999 --> 00:25:25,175
[instrumental music]

546
00:25:31,704 --> 00:25:33,662
And judging from your
reactions, none of you did that.

547
00:25:33,749 --> 00:25:35,272
Of course we didn't.

548
00:25:35,359 --> 00:25:38,928
Well, that clarifies
things considerably.

549
00:25:39,015 --> 00:25:40,800
How?

550
00:25:42,453 --> 00:25:45,544
Well, either one
of you framed Bret Stiles..

551
00:25:45,631 --> 00:25:47,894
Or?

552
00:25:47,981 --> 00:25:49,983
Or you didn't.

553
00:25:51,550 --> 00:25:53,247
What does that mean?

554
00:25:53,334 --> 00:25:55,423
Sorry. Can't tell you that.
That is a police secret.

555
00:25:55,510 --> 00:25:59,775
But you're gonna love it
when you do find out.

556
00:25:59,862 --> 00:26:01,821
Carry on.

557
00:26:10,743 --> 00:26:12,353
[crickets chirping]

558
00:26:12,440 --> 00:26:15,051
[Grace Van Pelt]
'You said you'd fix it.'

559
00:26:15,138 --> 00:26:17,619
Do you have hot water
at your place?

560
00:26:17,706 --> 00:26:20,274
Yeah, it's nice, isn't it?

561
00:26:20,361 --> 00:26:21,928
Fine. Whatever.

562
00:26:26,106 --> 00:26:28,891
[Grace gasping]

563
00:26:31,981 --> 00:26:34,854
[soft music]

564
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:46,561
Hmm.

565
00:26:46,648 --> 00:26:48,737
Well, what do you know?

566
00:26:48,824 --> 00:26:50,826
[Rigsby]
'Farragut had a commune
called Visualize'

567
00:26:50,913 --> 00:26:52,654
'down at Big Sur
in the mid '70s.'

568
00:26:52,741 --> 00:26:54,221
'Just a few hundred members'

569
00:26:54,308 --> 00:26:57,616
and a promising young
guru named Bret Stiles.

570
00:26:57,703 --> 00:27:00,183
Farragut crashes his car.
Stiles takes over.

571
00:27:00,270 --> 00:27:02,316
Now, the police report says
it was an accident.

572
00:27:02,403 --> 00:27:04,666
Randal Parker says no,
Stiles killed Farragut

573
00:27:04,753 --> 00:27:06,450
and the cop that made
the accident report

574
00:27:06,537 --> 00:27:08,670
and Meadows, who was
investigating the whole thing.

575
00:27:08,757 --> 00:27:10,759
That's crazy.

576
00:27:10,846 --> 00:27:12,543
I don't know. I wouldn't
put anything past Stiles.

577
00:27:12,631 --> 00:27:14,415
Well, one thing's
for certain. We can't prove it.

578
00:27:14,502 --> 00:27:17,723
But Meadows may have a source
inside Visualize who can.

579
00:27:17,810 --> 00:27:19,855
- Any idea who that source is?
- No.

580
00:27:19,942 --> 00:27:22,771
It's definitely not
the sister. Who else is there?

581
00:27:22,858 --> 00:27:25,513
Maybe Stiles knows.

582
00:27:25,600 --> 00:27:27,471
I could ask. He talked
to me earlier.

583
00:27:27,558 --> 00:27:28,864
Maybe I could get
him to open up.

584
00:27:28,951 --> 00:27:31,693
What'd he say?

585
00:27:31,780 --> 00:27:34,043
He showed me something.

586
00:27:34,130 --> 00:27:36,611
Never mind. It's nothing.

587
00:27:36,698 --> 00:27:38,395
He's trying to get to you.

588
00:27:38,482 --> 00:27:39,701
What?

589
00:27:39,788 --> 00:27:41,268
No.

590
00:27:41,355 --> 00:27:44,575
Okay. Take another run
at him, but be careful.

591
00:27:44,663 --> 00:27:46,099
Alright.

592
00:27:56,065 --> 00:27:58,241
Hello, Grace.
You look refreshed.

593
00:27:58,328 --> 00:27:59,982
You tried my little trick then.

594
00:28:00,069 --> 00:28:01,331
No.

595
00:28:01,418 --> 00:28:04,247
Works though, doesn't it?

596
00:28:04,334 --> 00:28:06,380
I wanted to ask you
about Timothy Farragut.

597
00:28:06,467 --> 00:28:08,164
He ran Visualize
before you did, right?

598
00:28:08,251 --> 00:28:11,907
No one ran Visualize
before I did.

599
00:28:11,994 --> 00:28:13,343
No one.

600
00:28:13,430 --> 00:28:14,997
Hmm.

601
00:28:15,084 --> 00:28:17,347
Tim said things that
people wanted to hear

602
00:28:17,434 --> 00:28:20,481
but he didn't know what to do
with the people who listened.

603
00:28:20,568 --> 00:28:21,787
What do you mean?

604
00:28:21,874 --> 00:28:24,441
[sighs]
Farragut was a dreamer.

605
00:28:24,528 --> 00:28:26,443
And dreamers are
useful in their way

606
00:28:26,530 --> 00:28:29,142
but we don't live
in dreams, do we?

607
00:28:29,229 --> 00:28:32,406
We live in the here
and the now.

608
00:28:32,493 --> 00:28:33,886
Did you kill him?

609
00:28:35,975 --> 00:28:38,325
You've killed, haven't you?

610
00:28:38,412 --> 00:28:41,937
Someone who mattered
to you, who hurt you.

611
00:28:42,024 --> 00:28:44,766
Did you enjoy it?

612
00:28:44,853 --> 00:28:48,465
- No.
- Really?

613
00:28:48,552 --> 00:28:51,033
I'm told it gives the feeling
of great perspective

614
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:53,427
like waking from a dream.

615
00:28:55,603 --> 00:28:58,519
It...it changes you.

616
00:28:59,738 --> 00:29:01,827
Did your friends notice?

617
00:29:03,480 --> 00:29:05,134
A little.

618
00:29:05,221 --> 00:29:06,919
But I think
it freaks 'em out.

619
00:29:07,006 --> 00:29:09,965
Of course. They don't
have the imagination

620
00:29:10,052 --> 00:29:14,927
orthe vocabulary to converse
with you about it. Hmm.

621
00:29:15,014 --> 00:29:17,277
You know..

622
00:29:17,364 --> 00:29:19,888
...you're like a little
bird that fell out of a nest

623
00:29:19,975 --> 00:29:22,412
but you don't need the nest.

624
00:29:22,499 --> 00:29:24,719
You need to fly..

625
00:29:24,806 --> 00:29:27,330
...and that..

626
00:29:27,417 --> 00:29:29,506
...I can teach you.

627
00:29:34,685 --> 00:29:36,383
[Lisbon]
'How was your trip
to Visualize?'

628
00:29:36,470 --> 00:29:39,038
Enlightening. I need a
little more information.

629
00:29:39,125 --> 00:29:41,170
Could Bret Stiles know what
blood type Meadows was?

630
00:29:41,257 --> 00:29:43,477
Sure. Visualize keeps medical
records on all its members.

631
00:29:43,564 --> 00:29:45,871
And the source for Meadows'
stories was highly placed?

632
00:29:45,958 --> 00:29:47,176
Way high.

633
00:29:47,263 --> 00:29:48,438
Great.

634
00:29:48,525 --> 00:29:49,875
Do you know
who the killer is?

635
00:29:49,962 --> 00:29:52,703
Better. I know
how to catch them.

636
00:29:52,791 --> 00:29:55,271
We need Stiles' lawyer in here
first thing in the morning.

637
00:29:55,358 --> 00:29:56,838
Did you get anything
from Stiles?

638
00:29:56,925 --> 00:29:59,972
No. He's not saying anything.

639
00:30:00,059 --> 00:30:01,538
Van Pelt, you know how
to do that computer

640
00:30:01,625 --> 00:30:03,018
picture makeover thing?

641
00:30:03,105 --> 00:30:04,280
You mean Photoshop?

642
00:30:04,367 --> 00:30:05,934
Yeah. We got
a lot of work to do.

643
00:30:06,021 --> 00:30:07,457
On what?

644
00:30:07,544 --> 00:30:09,503
Manufacturing evidence,
of course.

645
00:30:10,591 --> 00:30:13,507
[instrumental music]

646
00:30:17,772 --> 00:30:20,209
One of our people was
canvassing the neighborhood

647
00:30:20,296 --> 00:30:21,907
and noticed a
traffic light camera

648
00:30:21,994 --> 00:30:24,387
across from the A-CAN building.

649
00:30:24,474 --> 00:30:27,086
We blew up an image
and hit pay dirt.

650
00:30:27,173 --> 00:30:31,090
That looks like blood
to me. What do you think?

651
00:30:31,177 --> 00:30:33,048
Mr. Stiles?

652
00:30:35,921 --> 00:30:37,139
Bret?

653
00:30:38,662 --> 00:30:41,796
Meadows got evidence
that you killed Farragut.

654
00:30:41,883 --> 00:30:43,102
That's what was
on his computer.

655
00:30:43,189 --> 00:30:46,061
So you killed him
and stole it.

656
00:30:46,148 --> 00:30:48,368
But it was the cover-up
that got you.

657
00:30:50,718 --> 00:30:52,111
We're filing charges
against your client

658
00:30:52,198 --> 00:30:54,200
and booking him immediately.

659
00:30:54,287 --> 00:30:55,810
I'll stay with him, of course.

660
00:30:55,897 --> 00:30:58,073
It's alright, Jon. Bye.

661
00:30:58,160 --> 00:31:00,380
- But, Bret, I think--
- Jon.

662
00:31:08,257 --> 00:31:11,130
[intense music]

663
00:31:27,755 --> 00:31:30,889
A phony photograph, Patrick?

664
00:31:30,976 --> 00:31:32,368
Worth a thousand lies.

665
00:31:32,455 --> 00:31:34,631
So you've stooped
to framing me now?

666
00:31:34,718 --> 00:31:36,938
Well, you framed yourself first.

667
00:31:37,025 --> 00:31:38,461
Well. That's true. I did.

668
00:31:38,548 --> 00:31:40,028
But you never
believed it anyway.

669
00:31:40,115 --> 00:31:41,421
Well, I didn't think
you were that stupid

670
00:31:41,508 --> 00:31:43,510
unless, of course, you
were doing it on purpose.

671
00:31:43,597 --> 00:31:45,033
Ah. It was a pity.

672
00:31:45,120 --> 00:31:46,992
One of my better suits.

673
00:31:47,079 --> 00:31:48,776
Shame to have to ruin
it like that.

674
00:31:48,863 --> 00:31:50,560
Where'd you get the blood?

675
00:31:50,647 --> 00:31:53,041
Oh, I have my sources.

676
00:31:53,128 --> 00:31:55,478
The DNA won't match,
of course, but by the time

677
00:31:55,565 --> 00:31:58,742
that comes to light, I'll
know what I need to know.

678
00:31:58,829 --> 00:32:00,919
Which is which one
of the inner circle

679
00:32:01,006 --> 00:32:02,398
wanted to take your place.

680
00:32:02,485 --> 00:32:04,487
Well, it's best
to smoke out your rivals

681
00:32:04,574 --> 00:32:06,576
before they get too strong.

682
00:32:06,663 --> 00:32:08,622
I appeared weak

683
00:32:08,709 --> 00:32:12,539
because I wanted to see
who would reach for the crown.

684
00:32:12,626 --> 00:32:15,150
- Who did?
- No one.

685
00:32:15,237 --> 00:32:17,892
You overestimated the courage
of your bootlickers.

686
00:32:17,979 --> 00:32:20,068
They're all too scared
to make a move.

687
00:32:20,155 --> 00:32:24,159
Oh. That'swhy you doctored up
that ridiculous photograph.

688
00:32:24,246 --> 00:32:26,640
Well, I figured if they
wanted to cook your goose

689
00:32:26,727 --> 00:32:29,164
a little better, I'd turn
up the heat for them.

690
00:32:29,251 --> 00:32:32,254
Well, thank you, Patrick.
I didn't know you cared.

691
00:32:32,341 --> 00:32:34,126
I don't.

692
00:32:34,213 --> 00:32:36,389
I did it to catch
the killer. You coming?

693
00:32:36,476 --> 00:32:38,304
- Where are we going?
- Crash the party.

694
00:32:38,391 --> 00:32:40,175
Oh, I love a party.

695
00:32:51,970 --> 00:32:53,580
I nominate Jason Cooper

696
00:32:53,667 --> 00:32:56,713
to assume all roles and
duties as head of Visualize.

697
00:32:58,802 --> 00:33:01,283
I second the motion.

698
00:33:01,370 --> 00:33:06,201
Then we vote. I vote "aye."

699
00:33:06,288 --> 00:33:09,204
Well, I will not
allow false modesty

700
00:33:09,291 --> 00:33:11,337
to tarnish the moment.

701
00:33:11,424 --> 00:33:12,860
Aye.

702
00:33:12,947 --> 00:33:15,080
Aye.

703
00:33:15,167 --> 00:33:17,647
Nay.

704
00:33:17,734 --> 00:33:22,783
Bret Stiles isand always will
be the leader of Visualize.

705
00:33:22,870 --> 00:33:24,350
Nay.

706
00:33:28,832 --> 00:33:30,486
Aye.

707
00:33:36,884 --> 00:33:39,800
[people applauding]

708
00:33:44,109 --> 00:33:46,633
Uh, I'm sorry. Are we
interrupting something?

709
00:33:49,027 --> 00:33:51,116
Bret, you're free.

710
00:33:51,203 --> 00:33:52,769
We dropped the charges.

711
00:33:52,856 --> 00:33:55,163
Looks like you guys
have been busy.

712
00:33:55,250 --> 00:33:57,644
Hello, Cooper.

713
00:33:57,731 --> 00:34:01,735
I see you've taken the seat
at the head of the table.

714
00:34:03,606 --> 00:34:05,478
That's very bold of you.

715
00:34:06,957 --> 00:34:08,133
We thought..

716
00:34:08,220 --> 00:34:09,612
What? You thought, what?

717
00:34:09,699 --> 00:34:12,137
My corpse cold enough to loot?

718
00:34:12,224 --> 00:34:13,529
Bret.

719
00:34:13,616 --> 00:34:15,270
Mr. Stiles..

720
00:34:15,357 --> 00:34:19,100
Do you really think that I am
weak enough to be pushed aside?

721
00:34:19,187 --> 00:34:21,363
I mean, do-do you think
that I'm stupid enough

722
00:34:21,450 --> 00:34:24,323
to get caught
with blood on my hands?

723
00:34:29,328 --> 00:34:31,634
Tell me, Cooper.

724
00:34:31,721 --> 00:34:34,246
Do I seem weak to you now?

725
00:34:36,596 --> 00:34:38,511
Hmm?

726
00:34:38,598 --> 00:34:40,034
Sit down.

727
00:34:44,473 --> 00:34:46,910
[people applauding]

728
00:34:46,997 --> 00:34:48,999
Thank you.

729
00:34:49,087 --> 00:34:50,479
Nice to be back.

730
00:34:50,566 --> 00:34:53,047
Just one more piece
of business to, uh, clear up.

731
00:34:53,134 --> 00:34:55,005
In the matter
of personal curiosity

732
00:34:55,093 --> 00:34:57,399
who of you voted
for Cooper here?

733
00:34:58,008 --> 00:34:59,009
Hmm?

734
00:35:00,707 --> 00:35:03,057
Well, I don't see
how that's important.

735
00:35:03,144 --> 00:35:04,537
Well, that's one vote,

736
00:35:04,624 --> 00:35:07,453
Obviously. Who else
voted for Cooper?

737
00:35:09,411 --> 00:35:11,935
You did.

738
00:35:12,022 --> 00:35:14,851
And you.

739
00:35:14,938 --> 00:35:16,636
'Yes, and you.'

740
00:35:16,723 --> 00:35:19,769
Oh, Bret, looks like
it was a landslide.

741
00:35:19,856 --> 00:35:21,467
Well, Leben.

742
00:35:21,554 --> 00:35:24,165
You've always been
true to me, I know.

743
00:35:27,560 --> 00:35:28,865
Hmm.

744
00:35:29,997 --> 00:35:31,346
I see.

745
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:35,394
I stood by you, Bret.

746
00:35:35,481 --> 00:35:38,136
I told them they were
fools to doubt you.

747
00:35:38,701 --> 00:35:40,442
Really?

748
00:35:40,529 --> 00:35:42,662
Stiles is going down for murder.

749
00:35:42,749 --> 00:35:44,316
Shouldn't you have sucked
up to the new boss

750
00:35:44,403 --> 00:35:46,318
'like the rest of these folks?'

751
00:35:46,405 --> 00:35:48,450
The bloody clothes,
the security footage

752
00:35:48,537 --> 00:35:50,931
'Stiles seemed very guilty.'

753
00:35:51,018 --> 00:35:53,977
You had to be 100%
sure he wasn't.

754
00:35:54,064 --> 00:35:55,849
How would you know that?

755
00:35:58,460 --> 00:36:02,551
You're the killer.

756
00:36:02,638 --> 00:36:04,423
That's ridiculous.

757
00:36:04,510 --> 00:36:07,295
- Hmm.
- Is it?

758
00:36:07,382 --> 00:36:08,905
[Lisbon]
'You couldn't warn the others'

759
00:36:08,992 --> 00:36:11,256
'because then they would
know you were a murderer.'

760
00:36:11,343 --> 00:36:14,563
You voted for Stiles because
you knew he'd be coming back.

761
00:36:16,739 --> 00:36:18,741
[intense music]

762
00:36:24,269 --> 00:36:26,706
Damn you for what
you've turned me into.

763
00:36:31,232 --> 00:36:34,235
So you were Meadows'
inside source?

764
00:36:34,322 --> 00:36:36,846
I fed him information
when it helped me.

765
00:36:36,933 --> 00:36:39,371
You leaked stories that hurt
your enemies at Visualize.

766
00:36:43,462 --> 00:36:47,379
My mistake was..

767
00:36:47,466 --> 00:36:49,729
...was letting him
know who I was.

768
00:36:51,948 --> 00:36:54,081
Meadows was desperate

769
00:36:54,168 --> 00:36:56,388
to connect Stiles
to the murder of Farragut

770
00:36:56,475 --> 00:36:59,782
and he thought I knew
something that could prove it.

771
00:36:59,869 --> 00:37:02,698
Why not give it to him?

772
00:37:02,785 --> 00:37:04,918
Doesn't exist.

773
00:37:06,485 --> 00:37:10,793
Do you think Bret Stiles
needs a smoking gun?

774
00:37:10,880 --> 00:37:15,581
He probably didkill
Farragut and that sheriff

775
00:37:15,668 --> 00:37:18,148
but there'll never be
any evidence to prove it.

776
00:37:19,411 --> 00:37:20,934
I don't believe you.

777
00:37:21,021 --> 00:37:22,544
That's the truth.

778
00:37:29,247 --> 00:37:30,726
What do you think Stiles
would do if he found out

779
00:37:30,813 --> 00:37:32,554
you'd been feeding
me information?

780
00:37:35,035 --> 00:37:37,429
[Nora Hill]
I sensed that Meadows' crusade

781
00:37:37,516 --> 00:37:40,736
was pushing him
to desperate measures.

782
00:37:40,823 --> 00:37:44,523
So...I brought the
hammer, just in case.

783
00:37:58,145 --> 00:38:01,322
I knew my name was all over
the laptop, so I took it.

784
00:38:06,936 --> 00:38:08,851
I examined myself honestly

785
00:38:08,938 --> 00:38:12,246
and I find I feel no remorse.

786
00:38:14,292 --> 00:38:17,382
150,000 people die
every single day.

787
00:38:17,469 --> 00:38:19,340
And why should I feel anything

788
00:38:19,427 --> 00:38:21,516
for one man, just 'cause..

789
00:38:26,521 --> 00:38:28,958
...just 'cause I was
the cause of his death?

790
00:38:30,569 --> 00:38:32,484
That's a good question.

791
00:38:32,571 --> 00:38:33,963
Sign here.

792
00:38:42,450 --> 00:38:44,626
So that's it then.

793
00:38:44,713 --> 00:38:47,107
Thank you for letting
me watch this.

794
00:38:47,194 --> 00:38:49,152
No problem.

795
00:38:49,239 --> 00:38:52,373
Visualizing my anger
works really well.

796
00:38:52,460 --> 00:38:54,332
Just returning the favor.

797
00:38:57,378 --> 00:39:00,338
There's so much more
that I could teach you.

798
00:39:00,425 --> 00:39:02,035
Is there?

799
00:39:02,122 --> 00:39:05,734
To be free of doubt,
anger, fear.

800
00:39:08,650 --> 00:39:11,653
Let me show you the world
as you've never seen it.

801
00:39:11,740 --> 00:39:13,046
Let me show you

802
00:39:13,133 --> 00:39:16,354
you as you've never
seen yourself.

803
00:39:19,922 --> 00:39:21,794
Great sales pitch.

804
00:39:24,405 --> 00:39:27,277
But I'm sort of over
bad boys these days.

805
00:39:39,507 --> 00:39:41,553
Thanks anyway.

806
00:39:49,517 --> 00:39:51,476
Ms. Hill killed Gabe? Why?

807
00:39:51,563 --> 00:39:54,696
There was political
stuff going on.

808
00:39:54,783 --> 00:39:58,918
But I think mostly your brother
just wanted you to be happy

809
00:39:59,005 --> 00:40:00,310
and to get out.

810
00:40:00,398 --> 00:40:02,661
I don't know what to do.

811
00:40:02,748 --> 00:40:04,576
We were going through
your brother's laptop

812
00:40:04,663 --> 00:40:07,535
and we found some videos
that he'd made for you.

813
00:40:08,797 --> 00:40:10,408
Kinda like letters.

814
00:40:12,061 --> 00:40:14,412
I thought you might
like to see one.

815
00:40:16,152 --> 00:40:17,980
'Happy Birthday, sis.'

816
00:40:18,067 --> 00:40:20,026
I hope it's a good one.

817
00:40:20,113 --> 00:40:21,941
I don't know if you'll
ever see this

818
00:40:22,028 --> 00:40:25,901
but I just wanted to say
that I'm proud of you.

819
00:40:25,988 --> 00:40:29,252
'I miss you so much, sis.'

820
00:40:29,339 --> 00:40:31,167
'I know we don't agree
about some things'

821
00:40:31,254 --> 00:40:35,607
but, uh, I hope you know
that I'm always here for you.'

822
00:40:35,694 --> 00:40:36,825
Anytime.

823
00:40:38,392 --> 00:40:40,525
I'll be waiting.

824
00:40:40,612 --> 00:40:42,091
'See you soon, I hope.'

825
00:40:45,660 --> 00:40:47,053
Are there more of these?

826
00:40:47,140 --> 00:40:51,187
Yeah, there's a whole
lot of 'em.

827
00:40:51,274 --> 00:40:54,452
Would it be okay if I
stayed to watch a few?

828
00:40:54,539 --> 00:40:56,454
You can stay as
long as you'd like.

829
00:41:05,027 --> 00:41:06,942
Ah, Patrick.

830
00:41:07,029 --> 00:41:10,119
Always a pleasure
doing business with you.

831
00:41:10,206 --> 00:41:14,080
There are many fearful men
hiding from my wrath tonight.

832
00:41:14,167 --> 00:41:15,995
It's a good feeling

833
00:41:16,082 --> 00:41:18,476
And the murder of Farragut goes
back in the cold case file

834
00:41:18,563 --> 00:41:19,999
so that's a nice twofer.

835
00:41:20,086 --> 00:41:23,437
Well, you didn't do
toobadly yourself.

836
00:41:23,524 --> 00:41:26,527
You played a deep
game here, Patrick.

837
00:41:26,614 --> 00:41:29,748
A mind like yours could've
developed a dozen ways

838
00:41:29,835 --> 00:41:31,314
of tricking Nora Hill

839
00:41:31,401 --> 00:41:33,882
without helping me
in mylittle power play.

840
00:41:33,969 --> 00:41:36,581
Well, I'm flattered.
It was the easiest way.

841
00:41:36,668 --> 00:41:38,887
No.

842
00:41:39,932 --> 00:41:41,107
No.

843
00:41:42,674 --> 00:41:46,112
You wanted me to owe you
a favor, didn't you?

844
00:41:47,940 --> 00:41:50,246
Hmm? Well, alright.

845
00:41:50,333 --> 00:41:51,683
I owe you.

846
00:41:51,770 --> 00:41:53,293
Yes.

847
00:41:56,992 --> 00:41:59,125
You do.

848
00:42:02,128 --> 00:42:03,259
Goodnight.

849
00:42:03,346 --> 00:42:04,957
Goodnight.

850
00:42:07,046 --> 00:42:09,918
[instrumental music]

